#N Hexadecimal 十六进制显示器
#C Source URL: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AlephAlpha/golly/dcbc08738f07532dc8dbbf74d83b9d52a29a6953/Patterns/HashLife/Metacell/hexadecimal.mc.gz
#C Source SHA256: a9065c9343b193246459978fc6b494c35607b62aeb8e46390adec4768e5ab654
#C Offline conversion according to https://golly.sourceforge.io/Help/formats.html
#C Original format: [M2] (golly 1.2)
#C HexaDecimal.mc.gz
#C Two-digit hexadecimal counter in Conway's Life.  Distant relative
#C of Alan Hensel's Decimal Counter (see Guns/golly-ticker.rle),
#C and a close relative of the 'META' patterns in Hashing-Examples:
#C the tiles have the same base period, 35328 ticks, and share the
#C same large-scale pixel machinery, with an adapted clock mechanism.
#C The first pixel updates begin just before generation 6000.
#C
#C The tiles can easily be programmed to count from 00 to FF by ones,
#C but the novelty wears off quickly!  The original version started
#C out counting by ones; this version counts by &H10 up to &HD0 first.
#C Brice Due, 9 March 2007; movie reduced to 128 bits by Dave Greene.
#C
#C This pattern should be run in hashing mode, with a step size
#C of 8^4 or 8^5.  For this pattern, it's particularly important
#C that the 'Maximum memory for hashing' setting (File > Preferences,
#C on the Control tab) does not exceed your available RAM.  Using
#C virtual memory and paging hash information to the hard drive will
#C by far outweigh any speed advantages due to the larger hash table.
#C Original author: Brice Due; compressed movie by Dave Greene (2007)
#C Download SHA256: 53d7c77b718ccb2d265e18bc9c79ded081b97ec9f1556cf54973edb97ec4be3b
